WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.380
Clients, pricing and a UI portfolio that proves your craft.

2
00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:08.790
This is the video for module three of UI Design with Figma.

3
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:20.680
Most UI projects that go wrong were unclear from the first conversation. A client says I need an app design, and means something very different from what you imagine.

4
00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:30.060
Discovery is a short, structured call that turns a vague request into a defined list of screens and deliverables you can price.

5
00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:40.460
Ask about brand: is there a logo, colours, fonts? And users: what are the three most important things they do, and has anyone mapped the flows?

6
00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:45.910
Ask which platforms and phones, and who supplies the text and photos.

7
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:54.610
Ask who is building it and with what. Also ask for one decision-maker and one combined list of comments per round.

8
00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:08.300
For example, Kemi runs a tailoring studio in Ibadan and wants an Android app. She has a logo and two colours, but no wireframes. So the quote must include simple user flows first.

9
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:17.200
Her three tasks become 10 screens, plus 4 state frames, like an empty orders list and a booking slot unavailable.

10
00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:25.490
The inventory lists 7 components, and a spec page for handoff, because her developer has no paid Figma seat.

11
00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:32.140
Send a one-page summary the same day and ask the client to confirm it before any quote.

12
00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:45.660
Watch for red flags. Make it like a big company's app means dozens of screens, so ask which three matter most. Content later means sample content, with replacing it as a separate task.

13
00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:56.060
Each extra platform multiplies screens. A logo is a separate project. And no named decision-maker means endless feedback, unless you set limits.

14
00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:10.410
Weight each screen by effort. As a starting scale, a simple screen is about 3 hours, a medium one about 5, and a complex form or checkout about 8. Track your own time to make these honest.

15
00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:17.460
Then add the work that is not screens: foundations and components, the prototype, and handoff.

16
00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:26.280
Add a buffer for unknowns. Kemi's app comes to 91 hours, plus 15 percent, about 105 hours.

17
00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:38.880
At an example rate of 10,000 naira an hour, 105 hours is 1,050,000 naira. Set your own rate from your costs, skill and local demand.

18
00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:50.780
Split it by milestone: a 50 percent deposit of 525,000 naira, 30 percent when key screens are approved, and 20 percent at handoff.

19
00:02:52.030 --> 00:03:05.000
Write the limits too: two rounds of feedback per stage, and a price for each new screen. A new medium screen here is 50,000 naira. If the client wants to save money, remove a line, not just the total.

20
00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:12.730
Protect your money. Be wary of a new client who sends more than agreed and asks you to refund the difference.

21
00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:23.330
A payment screenshot or SMS alert is not money. Confirm every payment inside your own bank or wallet app before you start or deliver.

22
00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:34.520
Never share your BVN, OTPs or banking PIN. And keep the file in your own Figma team, sharing view-only links, until the final payment.

23
00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:45.500
Reviewers look past the final screens to see how you work. Do screens use component instances? Are colours linked to styles, not loose hex values?

24
00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:53.230
Are layers named, and are states designed beyond the happy path? Does the prototype run without dead ends?

25
00:03:53.580 --> 00:04:00.240
So show two or three projects in depth, with one honest case study. If it is a practice concept, say so.

26
00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:07.440
To remember. Run discovery across five areas, and confirm the screen inventory in writing.

27
00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:13.460
Build prices from weighted screens, stage the payments, and confirm money in your own account.

28
00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:17.910
Then pick one target path and act on it every week for 90 days.

29
00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:29.020
Now practise. Run a practice discovery call, write a priced proposal with staged payments, and publish your first case study with a view-only Figma link.

30
00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:31.310
Continue with the lessons below.
